 -1\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/state.json" "file_sha256" "8fd059646fee128a0be8d4870e075778d91b477e7aaf1cde6772706b029726e6"} {"question" "q-112149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 9 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+9{x}_{3}-4{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-9{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=6{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variables. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 6{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{3} \\\\ 6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{4} \\\\ -3{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/state.json" "file_sha256" "8fd059646fee128a0be8d4870e075778d91b477e7aaf1cde6772706b029726e6"} {"question" "q-112149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc264429e4f25b6e170cfaf2a0df06ead3b3411fb1957d5ecd420df18f600a71"} {"question" "q-112149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/state.json" "file_sha256" "8fd059646fee128a0be8d4870e075778d91b477e7aaf1cde6772706b029726e6"} {"question" "q-112149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={\\,}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/state.json" "file_sha256" "8fd059646fee128a0be8d4870e075778d91b477e7aaf1cde6772706b029726e6"} {"question" "q-112149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B={[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/state.json" "file_sha256" "8fd059646fee128a0be8d4870e075778d91b477e7aaf1cde6772706b029726e6"} {"question" "e-4820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/412e5084f88d0eb270b0e8a1f5613f85e1cec2c9f89879ccda30ff4c0794fb3d.png) find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/example-4820.json" "file_sha256" "559654063dc194d588a43bd7e07327407ea14b51a96693e964838b554b773956"} {"question" "e-4820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/7bba2b6227c4fe42901e654d6025c54d7937c13743dfc74586fc72c767c31c06.png)\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/60eca2d19c167355d263c471cb876e181db49ab5ac65b27561e6fb95bcc21ec0.png)\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/example-4820.json" "file_sha256" "559654063dc194d588a43bd7e07327407ea14b51a96693e964838b554b773956"} {"question" "e-4821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/e85d4892dc0bad0c6019b0434eff3d140fcf654171f6fda480b109b324e7b443.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/example-4821.json" "file_sha256" "45891f5b3b80cd4a2443cb7911fae916746649d95a6438c65a1ed16d2a4d89fc"} {"question" "e-4821" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following system:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/367b1779ba31df69791a24ab485f1715cedb46f57e383053fd17a189c465d09a.png)\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ This implies that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/9b2704bbd684106b7c1c7dbe52eb957e65e6634d9adb6ba7fb415b0097af7e64.png)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/example-4821.json" "file_sha256" "45891f5b3b80cd4a2443cb7911fae916746649d95a6438c65a1ed16d2a4d89fc"} {"question" "e-4819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/5971723fe2da8b35c0c965b25bf084080b2602b52a1099b6c97f637ca62fb2c2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/example-4819.json" "file_sha256" "9b6a43987aa9311eed093d39ba4b193ea016f5e9263e294c301cc46f4cb87a3a"} {"question" "e-4819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A\\sim B,$ the equation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and $x_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4$ where $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/7c7c6711d4453e0e8365dbec263cf23e0c8e4bf05ff091ecc3bf9593797ff538.png)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/example-4819.json" "file_sha256" "9b6a43987aa9311eed093d39ba4b193ea016f5e9263e294c301cc46f4cb87a3a"} {"question" "e-4822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/assets/13a1fbdcd6620ab5ccc41283da5ded8996053f9a59fd8c820ea786837ae05222.png) find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/example-4822.json" "file_sha256" "3374b3bb7d2251e2b309522ee1e3ea810c6c73774bf029cdb3419fd0b1b0770b"} {"question" "e-4822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So $\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},$ which means that the basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e., $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\,\\}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/example-4822.json" "file_sha256" "3374b3bb7d2251e2b309522ee1e3ea810c6c73774bf029cdb3419fd0b1b0770b"} {"question" "q-35529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "2be2577ee1ba267f53955d6b5c3124dff956bbb2c349654560cd739d27d28835" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived judgment: the given null-space vector equals x3[-3,2,1,0]+x4[2,-5,0,1]. These vectors span the stated space and are independent because their last two coordinates force both coefficients to zero in any zero combination. Original option 4, the worked solution, and Correct grading agree." "bindings" {"problem" "1330f2344eb220076c022f9c979a8136d023d49f05423661bde89677d71d27d8" "worked_solution" "6b0e3b6a5108591a278a5f510b59982ce477d123249d3916f2c7c81a47e3f7ff" "original_field" "9e18a69e82e783b1d2233a1ccca55701c98d738ccd05acdfd1c2a0444a8c0c70" "reviewed_field" "538d1fea685374b5aee555db692efcc7d0e44a4f117953714a741b3f12cc1066" "raw_widget" "e095f283770d266b6ed4d0e5580f8c388ef90ee1980cf0457a9bf2505883a3eb"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-35529-before.png" "sha256" "a523bd383b748f97b78ec4c78eaeb2ee842d92a62d