s x1=x4, x2=-2x4, and x3=3x4. Thus all solutions are x4[1,-2,3,1], and this nonzero vector forms a basis. Original option 4, the worked solution, and Correct grading agree."} {"question" "q-34522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "2be2577ee1ba267f53955d6b5c3124dff956bbb2c349654560cd739d27d28835" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived judgment: the row-equivalent matrix gives x1+x3=0 and x2=0. The null space is therefore all x3[-1,0,1], whose nonzero generator forms a basis. Original option 0, the worked solution, and Correct grading agree." "bindings" {"problem" "4aa58e18062d9ecd4619a62215cf8054b3039f7fd3398f69a3e429cf46acb2c4" "worked_solution" "64f374732083227d090d0633819b10044bbb7a05214a9207278fff022b17cce8" "original_field" "3acefeab23dcf332bd80ce1bfcdda15426c2e8b371dd15a236321580cb87649f" "reviewed_field" "3b67baad5ae98e76ca41496f985ce304b7eeae87562349d41bc556b97c581bae" "raw_widget" "b80cc8de91552b5041bc849e00216b23acf992fea43d8696055f4681e70f5ed2"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-34522-before.png" "sha256" "7f28eb377f77fcad3900e566b75a4e4a6403ee0ade630f73018b8282ad6a2b96"} {"path" "history-q-34522.png" "sha256" "dd3859fdae5c9377e1fa6b5e2727729ea944229bde04117847790b5eb7352533"} {"path" "q-34522-after.json" "sha256" "7e97db47350151d85beff37ac5d0993357bd927c5e6c590cadd26aa5303765bf"}]} {"question" "q-34522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={\\,}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064941/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "2be2577ee1ba267f53955d6b5c3124dff956bbb2c349654560cd739d27d28835" "rationale" "Derived judgment: the row-equivalent matrix gives x1+x3=0 and x2=0. The null space is therefore all x3[-1,0,1], whose nonzero generator forms a basis. Original option 0, the worked solution, and Correct grading agree."}] "usage" {"q-35529" [] "e-4821" [] "e-4819" [] "q-34522" [] "e-4820" [] "q-35460" [] "q-35450" [] "e-4822" []} "basis" 2132 "retractions" [[17592186120321 :question/problem "Given that $Null(A) = {\\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{3} + 2x_{4} \\\\ 2x_{3} - 5x_{4} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}: x_{3}, x_{4} ∈ R}$, find a basis for $Null(A)$."] [17592186120321 :question/answer-fields 17592186118401] [17592186120315 :question/problem "Given that $Null(A) = {\\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{3} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}: x_{3} ∈ R}$, find a basis for $Null(A)$."] [17592186120315 :question/answer-fields 17592186118365] [17592186120314 :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186120314 :question/answer-fields 17592186118359] [17592186120276 :question/problem "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $Null(A)$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186120276 :question/answer-fields 17592186118131] [17592186119555 :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."] [17592186119555 :question/worked-solution "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-x_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."] [17592186119556 :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119556 :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 0; x_{3} - 4x_{4} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 4x_{4} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4x_{4} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}$. This implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."] [17592186119554 :question/problem "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 9 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119554 :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\nSince $A\\sim B$, the equation\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} = 0; x_{2} - \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} + \\frac{2}{3}x_{4} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and\n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4\n$$\n\nwhere $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} - \\frac{2}{3}x_{4} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} = x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x_{3}, x_{4} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."] [17592186119557 :question/problem "Given the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."] [17592186119557 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} = 0; x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0} \\}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\,\\}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118132 17592186118133 17592186118134 17592186118135 17592186118136 17592186118360 17592186118361 17592186118362 17592186118363 17592186118364 17592186118366 17592186118367 17592186118368 17592186118369 17592186118370 17592186118402 17592186118403 17592186118404 17592186118405 17592186118406] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118131 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118131 :answer-field/id] [17592186118131 :answer-field/key] [17592186118131 :answer-field/type] [17592186118359 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118359 :answer-field/id] [17592186118359 :answer-field/key] [17592186118359 :answer-field/type] [17592186118365 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118365 :answer-field/id] [17592186118365 :answer-field/key] [17592186118365 :answer-field/type] [17592186118401 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118401 :answer-field/id] [17592186118401 :answer-field/key] [17592186118401 :answer-field/type]